  "title": "Solving Linear Equations",
  "content": "<p><strong>Solving</strong> an equation means finding the value of the unknown that makes the equation true. Use <strong>inverse operations</strong> to isolate the variable, always doing the same to both sides.</p><p>For <strong>two-step equations</strong> like \\(3x + 5 = 20\\): undo the addition/subtraction first, then undo the multiplication/division.</p><p>For <strong>unknowns on both sides</strong> like \\(5x + 3 = 2x + 15\\): collect the variable terms on one side and constants on the other.</p><p>For <strong>equations with brackets</strong>: expand the brackets first, then solve as normal.</p><p>For <strong>equations with fractions</strong>: multiply every term by the LCD to clear the fractions.</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Solve</strong> \\(4x - 7 = 2x + 9\\)</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> Subtract \\(2x\\): \\(2x - 7 = 9\\)</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> Add 7: \\(2x = 16\\)</p><p><strong>Step 3:</strong> Divide by 2: \\(x = 8\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> \\(x = 8\\)</p>"
 },
